WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.160
welcome to my list of 21 more nooby

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:07.080
python habits, I'm also giving away

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.480
pycharm licenses so get your

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:12.420
#pycharm down in the comments to enter

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:14.340
nooby habits are things that noobs do

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:16.379
that doesn't mean they're wrong or bad

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:18.539
or that you can't or shouldn't use them

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:20.279
but nonetheless they tip off other

00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:22.020
developers to your inexperience and

00:00:20.279 --> 00:00:24.359
avoiding them tends to improve your code

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:25.859
so whether you're brand spanking new to

00:00:24.359 --> 00:00:27.779
python or just trying to keep that

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:28.800
awesome tech job you somehow got let's

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:30.720
dive in.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.239
nooby thing #1, manually

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.680
rounding inside a print statement, you

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:36.480
computed some value and it's time to

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.160
print it out, but it's a floating point

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.500
value, if you just print it out as is

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.480
it's too many digits, too much clutter

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.420
so you just round it, right?

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.579
instead just use a format specifier.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:50.219
#2, repeatedly converting to and

00:00:47.579 --> 00:00:51.960
from numpy arrays, often accidentally. in

00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:53.520
most data analysis situations the best

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.320
thing is usually to just immediately

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.420
convert your data into numpy arrays or

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.920
pandas data frames then just make sure

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.660
you stick to all numpy or pandas

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.460
functions, it's a pretty common mistake

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:04.320
to actually use a non-numpy function

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:06.060
like the built-in max which results in

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.159
even worse performance than using max

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:11.280
on a list. #3 manipulating

00:01:08.159 --> 00:01:13.140
paths as strings. for obvious reasons

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.820
it's very common to represent a path as

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.560
a string, so there's the obvious

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.540
temptation to do string operations to

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.939
manipulate the paths. but this approach

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:23.640
is highly error prone. instead consider

00:01:20.939 --> 00:01:25.259
using the built-in pathlib. pathlib

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.939
already has built-in most of the common

00:01:25.259 --> 00:01:29.220
file and directory manipulations that

00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:31.860
you would want to do. #4 writing

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:34.080
IO functions that take the path instead

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:35.759
of an IO object. there's nothing really

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.380
wrong with having a convenience function

00:01:35.759 --> 00:01:38.939
that does this but what you should

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:40.500
really do is have a separate function

00:01:38.939 --> 00:01:43.320
that does all of the writing operations.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:45.360
so this function does all of the logic

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:47.220
of writing to the file and this function

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.780
is just a convenient wrapper around it.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.280
the reason for doing this especially if

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:52.680
you're writing a library is that people

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:53.880
might not want to open a file, or they

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.579
might want to be writing to a different

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:58.979
kind of IO object like to a zip file, to

00:01:56.579 --> 00:02:00.360
a network connection, or to a string. by

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:01.920
providing this version of the function

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.899
you allow people to write to whatever

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.700
they want to instead of just to a file...

00:02:03.899 --> 00:02:07.380
and speaking of writing to a string, that

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.259
brings us to our next nooby habit

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:12.239
concatenating strings with plus. the plus

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:14.220
operation on a string copies the string

00:02:12.239 --> 00:02:16.140
so in this for loop I end up making 100

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:19.739
copies of a larger and larger string.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:21.720
instead use a StringIO object. this

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:22.920
uses a growing in-memory buffer to

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:25.200
minimize the number of copies of the

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:26.760
string data and it has an interface just

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.620
like a file object that you'd get from

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:30.060
open, and once you're ready to convert

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:32.700
the buffer back into an actual string

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:34.020
object, call getvalue. #6, put a

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.660
finger down if you've never done this

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:38.640
one, using eval as a parser.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:41.040
through dubious means you've got a

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.019
string with a data structure in it. just

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.860
pass it to eval, what could possibly go

00:02:43.019 --> 00:02:46.140
wrong?

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:48.480
well of course the answer is eval is bad

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:51.239
and you should feel bad for trying.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.580
okay okay eval does have some legitimate

00:02:51.239 --> 00:02:55.260
uses, but parsing data from a string

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:57.239
isn't one I would probably use in

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:58.920
production. instead consider using an

00:02:57.239 --> 00:03:01.260
actual parsing Library like the built-in

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:03.599
json. #7 and this one really

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:06.239
pains me to see, storing your function

00:03:03.599 --> 00:03:08.099
inputs and or outputs as global

00:03:06.239 --> 00:03:09.720
variables. if you're reading from a

00:03:08.099 --> 00:03:11.400
global and using it to change something

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.019
about how your function works, then

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.360
consider it might be better if it's just

00:03:13.019 --> 00:03:17.159
an input parameter, and if you're writing

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.319
to a global variable it should probably

00:03:17.159 --> 00:03:21.000
be somewhere in your return value.

00:03:19.319 --> 00:03:23.220
hey, while you're here, did you know that

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.800
I'm available for consulting? that's

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:27.480
right, self promo! my team does python

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:29.040
consulting, contracting, training, and

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:32.099
interview prep. if you're interested,

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:34.860
visit mcoding.io. #8 thinking

00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:36.360
"and" and "or" return bools. you may not have

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:38.159
ever printed out the results of an "and"

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:40.560
or an "or" because you typically just use

00:03:38.159 --> 00:03:41.640
them in "if" statements. but if you did you

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.040
might have been surprised at the results.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:46.319
you don't get a True or False in general.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:49.080
"or" returns the first truthy thing or

00:03:46.319 --> 00:03:51.120
the last falsy thing, and "and" returns the

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:53.900
first falsy thing or the last truthy

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:56.220
thing. #9 single letter

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:58.799
variables. there are of course cases

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:00.180
where single letter variables are fine, a

00:03:58.799 --> 00:04:02.040
single underscore to denote something unused,

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.019
calling a loop variable "i", common

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:06.540
conventions like x, y, and z coordinates,

00:04:04.019 --> 00:04:08.220
or even well-known math formulas. no, what

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:10.260
I'm talking about is a whole function

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:12.900
where everything is just a single letter

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:14.459
or maybe two. with just a few carefully

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:16.139
thought out variable names it would be

00:04:14.459 --> 00:04:19.139
so much easier to understand.

00:04:16.139 --> 00:04:22.079
#10 using both div and mod instead

00:04:19.139 --> 00:04:23.699
of divmod. if you're doing, like, math I

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:25.320
guess? there's a decent chance that

00:04:23.699 --> 00:04:28.020
you'll want to divide by a number and

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:29.340
know both the quotient and remainder

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:30.720
that's what divmod does, it gives you

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:32.699
both a quotient and the remainder

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:36.060
without repeating the calculation.

00:04:32.699 --> 00:04:37.680
#11 not knowing about properties. a

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:39.840
lot of noobs, especially people coming

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.880
from java, are in the habit of writing

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:44.160
getters and setters for every variable.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.540
the more pythonic thing is to just leave

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.880
the variable as part of the public

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.259
interface but sometimes we use getters

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.300
and setters because we want to actually

00:04:49.259 --> 00:04:53.160
do something in them. in that case having

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:55.259
a separate get and set function does

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:56.880
make sense, but doing that means that in

00:04:55.259 --> 00:04:58.680
order to access the value I'm now

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:00.780
calling functions set_x and get_x

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:02.820
instead of the more intuitive dot notation.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:05.520
well that's exactly what a property lets

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:08.400
us do. now because of descriptor magic we

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.900
can just say object.x equals 42 and that

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:12.419
will actually call the setter with the

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:15.120
value being 42. and on a related note

00:05:12.419 --> 00:05:17.400
#12 having expensive properties.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.139
cool cool you learned what a property is

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:21.000
and now it's time to save two characters

00:05:19.139 --> 00:05:23.100
on every function call by converting

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.979
them into properties. slow down there.

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:27.240
it's not about saving characters.

00:05:24.979 --> 00:05:29.340
anything that takes much longer than

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:31.620
just a normal attribute access shouldn't

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:32.639
be turned into a property. if you have a

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:35.520
function that might be very expensive

00:05:32.639 --> 00:05:38.039
make it look expensive.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:39.360
#13 inserting or deleting while

00:05:38.039 --> 00:05:40.979
iterating.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:43.020
here we're looping over the dictionary

00:05:40.979 --> 00:05:45.240
and while that iteration is in progress

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:46.860
we try to delete a key. for a dictionary,

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.660
doing this is going to raise a runtime

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:50.699
error, but for other data types

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:52.440
especially C extension types this

00:05:50.699 --> 00:05:54.720
might just do something very weird and

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:56.340
not throw any error. replacing or

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.440
modifying a value on the other hand is

00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:00.000
typically fine. another approach is to

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:02.100
keep a set of keys that you're going to

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.479
delete, loop over the dictionary and add

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:05.639
anything that you want to delete into

00:06:03.479 --> 00:06:08.280
the to_delete set. then once you're done

00:06:05.639 --> 00:06:10.199
delete all of the keys afterwards.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:13.680
#14 and I know this will be controversial,

00:06:10.199 --> 00:06:14.759
any use of filter or map. the reason is

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:16.259
that they encourage you to write code

00:06:14.759 --> 00:06:18.120
that looks like this.

00:06:16.259 --> 00:06:20.880
in general you can replace any filter

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:22.560
like thism and you can replace any map

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.539
like this,

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:25.740
these generator comprehensions are lazy

00:06:24.539 --> 00:06:27.960
just like filter and map and they

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:29.280
produce the same elements, and bonus

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:31.440
points if you were just immediately

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.240
going to convert that to a list. instead

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:34.620
you could use a list comprehension which

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:36.840
is even more readable.

