for two hours was bad for your voice? but

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:44.940
you're worth it, 14 down let's keep going.

00:06:42.479 --> 00:06:47.160
#15 defining too many or

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:48.780
inappropriate dunder methods. some of

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:50.639
them like init or hash make

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:53.160
perfect sense to define for most classes.

00:06:50.639 --> 00:06:54.960
but the Person class does not represent

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:56.340
any kind of number or thing that

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:58.620
naturally has a plus operation

00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:00.180
associated with it. but this code goes

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:01.979
ahead and defines iadd to make

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:04.259
plus-equals mean make these two people

00:07:01.979 --> 00:07:06.240
friends. don't use a dunder method to

00:07:04.259 --> 00:07:08.580
accomplish this task, just use a normal

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:12.000
function with a good name. #16

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:14.160
trying to parse HTML or XML using

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:15.419
regular expressions. it's not that you

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:16.979
made a mistake, that your regular

00:07:15.419 --> 00:07:18.180
expression is bad or that you're not

00:07:16.979 --> 00:07:19.620
smart enough to come up with the

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:22.500
complete one that handles all the edge

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:23.880
cases. it's literally impossible to

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:27.120
create a regular expression that can

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:29.520
parse HTML because HTML is not a regular

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:31.319
language. instead you're going to need an

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.960
actual parser. you could use something

00:07:31.319 --> 00:07:35.460
like Python's built-in HTML parser or

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:37.080
you could reach for a more full featured

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:39.000
third-party library like BeautifulSoup,

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:41.460
which of course has a parser inside of

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:43.319
it. #17 not knowing about raw string

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:45.599
literals. in most srings it doesn't matter,

00:07:43.319 --> 00:07:47.699
but specifically for Windows paths and

00:07:45.599 --> 00:07:50.280
in regular expressions, using literal

00:07:47.699 --> 00:07:51.840
backlashes is very common. plop down an "r"

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:54.060
in front of your string to make it a raw

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:55.680
string literal. raw strings ignore escape

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:57.360
sequences, making regular expressions

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:01.080
much easier to read.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:02.639
#18 thinking that super means parent. you

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:05.280
might think that the super call is going

00:08:02.639 --> 00:08:08.160
to call the Root's version of "f", and in

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:09.240
most cases you'd be right, but not in all

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:10.919
cases.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:13.319
and the reason is that someone could

00:08:10.919 --> 00:08:16.199
inherit from your class. this instance of

00:08:13.319 --> 00:08:17.880
C is also an instance of A but when we

00:08:16.199 --> 00:08:22.080
call "f" we see that the chain of super

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:23.759
calls goes from C to A to B to Root. in

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:26.699
particular this super call inside of A

00:08:23.759 --> 00:08:29.099
right after printing "A.f" sent us to

00:08:26.699 --> 00:08:31.319
the sibling class B, not to A's parent

00:08:29.099 --> 00:08:32.640
Root. I've got a whole video on super if

00:08:31.319 --> 00:08:35.760
you'd like to hear more.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:37.800
#19 passing structured data around as a

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:39.300
raw dictionary or tuple. you do some

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:41.339
calculation, take a measurement, or

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:42.539
summarize some data. if these values are

00:08:41.339 --> 00:08:44.580
meaningful and always need to stick

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:46.620
together, then most likely this should be

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:48.240
a class and you should give it a name. if

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:49.980
you were returning a dictionary before, a

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:51.720
data class is probably what you want, and

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:53.940
if you were returning a tuple, then a

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:56.339
NamedTuple is probably what you want.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:58.500
#20 using collections namedtuple instead

00:08:56.339 --> 00:09:00.240
of typing NamedTuple. back in the day

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:01.800
this was a best-practice because it

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:04.019
allowed you to give tuple elements good

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:05.820
names. but nowadays the typing NamedTuple

00:09:04.019 --> 00:09:07.860
is a much more readable way to do

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:09.720
the same thing. it actually looks like a

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:12.060
class definition, it's easy to read, and

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:13.620
doesn't rely on strings. and as always

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:16.500
saving the best for last, #21

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:18.660
import-time side effects.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:21.000
please please please don't print

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:22.620
something when I import your module. even

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:24.060
if your library can also be used as a

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:26.100
script and that's the behavior that you

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:28.500
want when you run the script, use the

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:30.360
"def main if name main" idiom to hide it

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:31.500
behind a "main" function. that's another

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:33.660
one where I've got a whole video

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:36.240
explaining the benefits of doing this.

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:38.220
and that's 21. you must really love the

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:40.140
sound of my voice, or maybe you just like

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:41.880
my content in which case don't forget to

